Title: "Proof and Consequence"
Id: "bb27977c-e17b-4082-b455-cb97dbdf1b6f"


Text: "A mathematical argument is far more than a sequence of assertions that
       happen to be true. It is a demonstrable guarantee that a specific
       conclusion is inexorably forced by accepted premises. Logic formalizes
       this necessity through two fundamentally distinct, complementary
       paradigms: *semantics*, which evaluates truth across abstract
       mathematical structures and interpretations, and *syntax*, which governs
       the mechanical derivation of symbolic formulas according to explicit
       rules of inference.

       The profound harmony between these two perspectives forms the foundation
       of mathematical epistemology. A mature proof operates fluently across
       this boundary: it harnesses structural intuition from semantics while
       maintaining the uncompromising syntactic discipline that guarantees
       freedom from error."
Id: "ef73644e-51dd-4baf-954d-ddaadf68f2af"


SectionTitle: "Consequence, Validity, and Equivalence"
Id: "065af088-3182-4cff-b43c-6b707fb9efc1"


TextDefinition: "Let $\Gamma$ be a collection of sentences and let $\varphi$ be a
                 sentence in a common formal language. We say that $\varphi$ is
                 a *semantic consequence* of $\Gamma$, written $\Gamma \models
                 \varphi$, if every interpretation (model) in which every
                 sentence of $\Gamma$ evaluates to true also makes $\varphi$
                 true.

                 Semantic consequence asserts that it is impossible for the
                 premises to be simultaneously true while the conclusion is
                 false. If no interpretation can satisfy all sentences in
                 $\Gamma$ simultaneously—that is, if $\Gamma$ is inconsistent—the
                 condition holds vacuously, and every sentence $\varphi$ in the
                 language is a semantic consequence of $\Gamma$. This phenomenon
                 is known classically as *ex falso quodlibet* (from a
                 contradiction, anything follows)."
Id: "d6351c2e-8774-461d-8365-533a09dd2951"


TextDefinition: "A sentence $\varphi$ is *valid* (or a *tautology* in
                 propositional contexts), written $\models \varphi$, if it is
                 true in every conceivable interpretation. Conversely, $\varphi$
                 is *satisfiable* if there exists at least one interpretation in
                 which it is true.

                 A theory $\Gamma$ is satisfiable if its axioms possess a shared
                 model. Satisfiability is an inherently global property: every
                 finite subcollection of axioms may possess a model without the
                 entire theory being satisfiable, although in first-order logic
                 the celebrated Compactness Theorem guarantees that global
                 satisfiability is equivalent to finite satisfiability. The
                 construction of explicit models remains mathematics' principal
                 technique for proving that an assertion is independent of a
                 given set of axioms."
Id: "41277ec7-a7ee-44ac-bb2e-9d9726088e35"


TextDefinition: "Two formulas $\varphi$ and $\psi$ are *logically equivalent*,
                 written $\varphi \equiv \psi$ or $\models \varphi \iff \psi$,
                 when they receive identical truth values under every common
                 interpretation and variable assignment.

                 Logical equivalence is a metalogical relationship between
                 sentences; it licenses the substitution of equivalents in any
                 extensional context without altering the truth value of the
                 enclosing formula. It must not be conflated with the object-
                 language equality relation $x = y$, which asserts that two
                 syntactic names designate the same entity within a domain."
Id: "345f73c4-8464-4554-8e3f-6c4e36c545df"


SectionTitle: "Derivations and Proof Methods"
Id: "c2a58271-331c-4b14-b657-13f3e9d4d66f"


TextDefinition: "Fix a formal deductive system equipped with a collection of
                 logical axioms and rules of inference. A *derivation* (or
                 formal deduction) of a sentence $\varphi$ from a set of
                 premises $\Gamma$ is a finite tree or sequence of sentences
                 $\varphi_1, \varphi_2, \dots, \varphi_n = \varphi$ such that
                 each $\varphi_i$ is either an axiom, an element of $\Gamma$, or
                 the direct result of applying an allowable inference rule to
                 earlier members of the sequence.

                 When such a derivation exists, we write
                 $\Gamma \vdash \varphi$. Whereas the relation
                 $\Gamma \models \varphi$ is semantic and concerns infinite
                 classes of interpretations, the relation
                 $\Gamma \vdash \varphi$ is purely syntactic and finite: a
                 computer algorithm can inspect and verify every step of a
                 formal derivation in finite time."
Id: "5f0b2406-0146-4015-91b9-8875e70395fb"


TextTheorem: "A deductive system is *sound* if every formally provable sentence
              is a semantic consequence: $\Gamma \vdash \varphi \implies \Gamma
              \models \varphi$. It is *complete* if every semantic consequence
              is formally provable: $\Gamma \models \varphi \implies \Gamma
              \vdash \varphi$.

              Soundness ensures that the formal rules never introduce falsity
              from true premises. Completeness, established for first-order
              logic by Kurt Gödel in 1929, is one of the crowning intellectual
              achievements of modern logic: it guarantees that truth across all
              mathematical models can always be captured by finite symbolic
              manipulation."
Id: "d694227c-82c0-433b-acc2-8223d8b7954c"


TextDefinition: "Mathematical proofs employ several foundational strategic
                 architectures:
                 1. *Direct Proof*: To establish $P \implies Q$, assume $P$ as a
                 temporary hypothesis and deduce $Q$ via inference rules. The
                 Deduction Theorem guarantees that this syntactic deduction
                 yields a proof of the implication $P \implies Q$.
                 2. *Proof by Contraposition*: To establish $P \implies Q$,
                 assume $\neg Q$ and derive $\neg P$. This strategy relies upon
                 the classical equivalence $(P \implies Q) \iff (\neg Q \implies
                 \neg P)$ and is particularly effective when the negation of $Q$
                 provides more constructive information than $P$.
                 3. *Proof by Contradiction (Reductio ad Absurdum)*: To establish
                 $P$, assume $\neg P$ and derive a logical contradiction
                 ($Q \land \neg Q$). In classical logic, this guarantees the truth
                 of $P$ through the law of excluded middle."
Id: "35da6ee6-020c-4586-968e-c8ca0c29d984"


Text: "A *proof by cases* (or disjunction elimination) establishes a conclusion
       $C$ under a set of alternative hypotheses $A_1, A_2, \dots, A_k$ whose
       disjunction $A_1 \lor A_2 \lor \dots \lor A_k$ is known to be true. To
       succeed, the argument must demonstrate that $A_i \implies C$ for each
       individual index $i$.

       The cases need not be mutually exclusive, but they must be strictly
       *exhaustive*: failure to account for an edge case invalidates the entire
       proof. In constructive mathematics, a proof by cases must provide an
       algorithm for determining which case holds and how the witness is
       extracted."
Id: "bb9955c6-a7ac-4c57-92ab-d2f5d0e8b283"


SectionTitle: "Definitions and Mathematical Practice"
Id: "fe8bed61-7f39-45dc-92aa-50272e52c082"


TextDefinition: "An *explicit definition* introduces a new symbol or syntactic
                 shorthand by equating it with an expression formed entirely
                 from previously established concepts. A legitimate definition
                 must satisfy Stanisław Leśniewski's dual criteria of
                 *eliminability* (any occurrence of the new symbol can be
                 systematically replaced by its definiens) and *non-creativity*
                 (the definition cannot enable the proof of any new theorem in
                 the original vocabulary).

                 An *implicit definition* or axiomatic specification
                 characterizes an entity by the system of relations it satisfies
                 with other objects. Implicit definitions are mathematically
                 permissible only when existence and uniqueness have been
                 rigorously established, ensuring that the defined entity is
                 neither impossible nor ambiguous."
Id: "ea09b276-1d50-471e-a821-738fe3172a3f"


TextDefinition: "Mathematical literature organizes assertions into conventional
                 hierarchical categories that reflect their expository function:
                 - *Theorem*: A major result of paramount importance,
                   representing a substantial mathematical advance or structural
                   milestone.
                 - *Proposition*: A significant mathematical assertion that is
                   of genuine interest in its own right, though less sweeping
                   than a primary theorem.
                 - *Lemma*: A preparatory result whose principal purpose is to
                   clarify, streamline, or isolate a difficult step in the proof
                   of a subsequent theorem.
                 - *Corollary*: An immediate or short deduction that follows
                   with minimal effort from a previously established theorem.
                 - *Scholium*: An explanatory remark, commentary, or historical
                   note clarifying the wider significance of a result."
Id: "bfe0b849-abd6-45f4-9979-5c67f50ae5d2"


TextDefinition: "A *conjecture* is a precisely formulated mathematical statement
                 that is believed to be true based on compelling heuristic
                 arguments, computational evidence, or structural symmetry, but
                 for which no valid formal proof yet exists.

                 No amount of empirical verification or inductive confirmation
                 can transform a conjecture into a theorem. Conversely, a single
                 *counterexample*—a single entity in the domain that violates
                 the conjectured condition—instantly and definitively disproves
                 a universal conjecture."
Id: "685ecb26-0195-4a43-a5cd-efe88b92004a"


Text: "Exemplary mathematical writing rigorously maintains the transparency of
       its logical dependencies. Before invoking a theorem, the author must
       explicitly verify that all required hypotheses are satisfied in the
       current context; before assigning a name to an object, its existence must
       be confirmed; and before employing the definite article 'the', uniqueness
       must be demonstrated.

       These practices are not mere stylistic pedantry. They are the essential
       safeguards that distinguish genuine deductive proof from persuasive yet
       fallacious speculation, preserving the certainty that is the hallmark of
       mathematical knowledge."
Id: "6275edcb-d9f4-469b-93ef-68d25b92ec52"
